    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d456-a2">

                <pb facs="0477" n="457" sameAs="frus:frus1864p3/pg457"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 2.</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Pruyn to the
                        Gorogio</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 129.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the Unittd States in
                                Japan</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">Kanagawa,
                            Yokohama</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-11-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 13, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>To the <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gorogio:</hi></p>
                <p>I have received your excellencies’ letter in relation to the withdrawal of the
                    letter of Ogasawara with much satisfaction, and I thank your excellency for
                    having promptly followed the advice I gave you a few days since.</p>
                <p>Your excellencies will recollect that I have written several letters urging this
                    step, and while I regret that you have not acted earlier, I hope the delay will
                    not prove prejudicial. I presume you do not wish to have the letter delivered to
                    you, but to have it considered as withdrawn.</p>
                <p>To this I assent, and will send you the letter itself if desired.</p>
                <p>With respect and esteem,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>ROBERT H. PRUYN, <hi rendition="#italic">Minister Resident of the United
                            States in Japan.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
